CAT = "jelecGeneral"; SEG = "GEN"
Q = [
("What is the stated purpose of the National Electrical Code?",
 "Practical safeguarding of people and property", ["An efficient design for electrical systems", "A training manual for apprentices", "Planning for future electrical expansion"],
 "The Code's purpose is the practical safeguarding of persons and property from hazards arising from the use of electricity. It says plainly that it is not a design specification or an instruction manual for untrained persons."),
("Who interprets the Code and approves equipment and installations?",
 "The authority having jurisdiction", ["The equipment manufacturer", "The licensed master electrician", "The nationally recognized lab"],
 "The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ), usually the electrical inspector, interprets the rules and approves equipment and installations. Testing labs list equipment, but approval rests with the AHJ."),
("A 480Y/277 V switchboard has exposed live parts on one side and a grounded concrete wall on the other (Condition 2). Minimum working depth?",
 "3 1/2 ft", ["3 ft", "4 ft", "5 ft"],
 "At 151 V to ground and above (up to 1000 V), Condition 1 is 3 ft, Condition 2 is 3 1/2 ft and Condition 3 is 4 ft. Concrete counts as grounded, which makes this Condition 2."),
("A branch circuit is made up of the conductors between the final overcurrent device and:",
 "The outlets", ["The service point", "The main disconnect", "The feeder panelboard"],
 "A branch circuit runs from the final overcurrent device protecting the circuit to the outlets. Conductors upstream of that device are feeders or service conductors."),
("Unless equipment is marked otherwise, a 40 A circuit with 8 AWG conductors is limited to which ampacity column?",
 "The 60°C column", ["The 75°C column", "The 90°C column", "The 105°C column"],
 "Terminations on circuits rated 100 A or less, or for 14 through 1 AWG conductors, are limited to 60°C ratings unless the equipment is listed and marked for 75°C. Higher insulation ratings can be used only for adjustment and correction."),
("A load that runs at its maximum current for this long or longer is a continuous load:",
 "3 hours", ["1 hour", "2 hours", "8 hours"],
 "Article 100 defines a continuous load as one where the maximum current is expected to continue for 3 hours or more. Continuous loads are sized at 125 percent."),
("Unless equipment is marked otherwise, terminations on a 150 A feeder are based on which temperature rating?",
 "75°C", ["60°C", "90°C", "105°C"],
 "For circuits rated over 100 A or conductors larger than 1 AWG, terminations are rated 75°C unless the equipment is listed for higher. The 90°C rating is for adjustment and correction only."),
("\"Within sight\" means visible and not more than this distance away:",
 "50 ft", ["25 ft", "75 ft", "100 ft"],
 "Article 100 defines within sight as visible and not more than 50 ft from the other equipment. The rule appears in motor, appliance and air-conditioning disconnect requirements."),
("What is the minimum working-space depth in front of a 120/240 V panelboard?",
 "3 ft", ["2 1/2 ft", "3 1/2 ft", "4 ft"],
 "For 0 to 150 V to ground, the working-space depth is 3 ft under all three conditions. Greater depths, such as 3 1/2 or 4 ft, apply from 151 V to ground upward."),
("Which of these is a damp location?",
 "An open porch under a roof", ["A raceway buried underground", "A slab in contact with earth", "An office on an upper floor"],
 "Damp locations are partially protected areas such as roofed open porches and canopies, and interiors with moderate moisture like some basements. Underground raceways and slabs on earth are wet locations; an office is dry."),
("Conductors between the service equipment and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device are:",
 "Feeder conductors", ["Branch-circuit conductors", "Service-entrance conductors", "Service-lateral conductors"],
 "Article 100 defines a feeder as the conductors between the service equipment (or a separately derived system or other source) and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device."),
("Informational notes in the NEC are:",
 "Explanatory and not enforceable", ["Mandatory once locally adopted", "Enforceable in dwellings only", "Enforceable for new work only"],
 "Informational notes explain or point to other material and are not enforceable as requirements of the Code. Only the rule text itself is mandatory."),
("Bonding means parts are connected so that they:",
 "Have electrical continuity", ["Are connected to the earth", "Are insulated from each other", "Carry the normal neutral current"],
 "Bonded (bonding) means connected to establish electrical continuity and conductivity. Grounding refers to a connection to earth; bonding is about joining metal parts so a fault can clear."),
("Which makes a disconnect NOT readily accessible?",
 "Needing a portable ladder to reach it", ["Being inside a utility closet", "Being mounted 5 ft above the floor", "Being behind an unlocked door"],
 "Readily accessible means it can be reached quickly without climbing over obstacles, removing things, or using portable ladders. An unlocked door or a utility room does not take away ready access."),
("The width of working space in front of electrical equipment must be at least:",
 "30 in. or the equipment width", ["24 in. or the equipment width", "36 in. regardless of width", "48 in. or the door width"],
 "Working space must be the width of the equipment or 30 in., whichever is greater, and must allow equipment doors to open at least 90 degrees."),
("Minimum headroom in the working space at a service panel, unless the equipment is taller, is:",
 "6 1/2 ft", ["6 ft", "7 ft", "8 ft"],
 "Working space must be clear from the floor to a height of 6 1/2 ft (2.0 m) or the height of the equipment, whichever is greater. Small existing dwelling services have a limited exception."),
("In Code language, the phrase \"shall be permitted\" means the action is:",
 "Allowed but not required", ["Mandatory in all cases", "Recommended but not enforced", "Required only for new work"],
 "Permissive rules use \"shall be permitted\" or \"shall not be required\" and describe actions that are allowed but optional. Mandatory rules use \"shall\" or \"shall not\"."),
("Large equipment needs a working-space entrance at each end when it is over 6 ft wide and rated at least:",
 "1200 A", ["400 A", "800 A", "2000 A"],
 "Equipment rated 1200 A or more and over 6 ft wide needs an entrance to the working space at each end, each at least 24 in. wide and 6 1/2 ft high, with listed exceptions."),
("Listed equipment must be installed and used according to:",
 "Its listing and labeling instructions", ["The engineer's specification only", "The supplier's catalog data", "Common local trade practice"],
 "Listed or labeled equipment must be installed and used in accordance with any instructions included in the listing or labeling. Catalogs and habits do not override the listing."),
("When equipment is marked with a terminal torque value, the connection must be made:",
 "With a calibrated torque tool", ["By hand until it feels snug", "With a cordless impact driver", "Until the conductor deforms"],
 "Where a tightening torque is indicated, a calibrated torque tool must be used to achieve it unless the manufacturer gives another method. Loose or overtightened lugs are a leading cause of failures."),
]
